# Sponza Modern's multi-GB pack set lives on an external SSD (BLIX_SPONZA_ASSETS),
# never in the repo. Guard against an accidental local copy getting committed.
# Assets/ = cooked (runtime); Assets-src/ = raw sources (setup's default split).
src/Blix.Demos.SponzaModern/Assets/
src/Blix.Demos.SponzaModern/Assets-src/

# Perf matrix logs: the plan is the record, the logs are the working notes.
perf/

# The glTF conformance corpus is a pinned DOWNLOAD, not a commit: the manifest beside
# it is the record, and three upstream licences stay out of this history. See
# third_party/gltf-corpus.manifest and tools/fetch-gltf-corpus.sh.
third_party/gltf-corpus/

# The lab baseline: 68 hashed artifacts, re-recordable in a minute by
# tools/lab-baseline.sh record. A control is a thing you re-derive, not a thing you commit.
.baseline/

# Cooked artifacts are DERIVED, so they are not committed — the rule .baseline/
# already follows: a thing you can re-derive in a minute is not a thing to carry
# in history.
#
# This was not always true. 75 .blixmesh and 4 .blixfont were tracked, and the
# cook arc made keeping them untenable: once a cooked mesh carried its own image
# table it also had to carry the images, and cooking the ones embedded in a .glb
# extracts and re-encodes them — BC7 is fixed-rate where the source was entropy-
# coded, so RTSGame's two villagers alone turned 67 MB of .glb into 118 MB of
# .blixtex. Committing that would have grown the repository by more than the
# assets it was storing.
#
# Not committing them also fixes a fresh clone rather than merely shrinking it.
# The cook target is incremental on Inputs/Outputs, and a committed output whose
# checkout timestamp is not reliably older than its input is a build that may or
# may not re-cook. An output that does not exist cannot be skipped.
*.blixmesh
*.blixtex
*.blixprobe
*.blixfont
*.textures/
